cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use DeepCatch in your research, please cite it as follows."
title: "An Open-Source Benchmark for Panel-Based Ultra-Sensitive Detection of Circulating Tumor DNA from Real Tumor Mutations"
authors:
  - given-names: "Yu Ching"
    family-names: "Lam"
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher (no institutional affiliation; verified email domain umich.edu)"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-9113-769X"
year: 2026
# There is no `doi:` key here yet, and that is deliberate. This
# repository has no Zenodo record of its own: the Zenodo API returns
# zero records for it, so the DOI of the v2.2.0 release was never
# minted either. A placeholder string under `doi:` would render in
# GitHub's "Cite this repository" box as a link to a record that does
# not exist. The `doi:` key is added, with the minted value, by the
# release that carries it -- see ZENODO_SETUP.md.
#
# There is no `identifiers:` entry for the same reason. It used to
# declare a DOI whose value was the string "to be assigned (preprint)":
# not a DOI at all, but sitting in a machine-readable field that says
# `type: doi`, so a citation tool would record it as one. The preprint's
# DOI is added when the preprint exists -- see ZENODO_SETUP.md section 5.
url: "https://github.com/rollroyces/deepcatch"
repository-code: "https://github.com/rollroyces/deepcatch"
repository-companion: "https://github.com/rollroyces/cfdna-fragmentomics-pipeline"
license: MIT
version: "2.3.0"
date-released: 2026-11-16
keywords:
  - "cfDNA"
  - "ctDNA"
  - "liquid biopsy"
  - "molecular residual disease (MRD)"
  - "panel LLR"
  - "fragmentomics"
  - "tumor-naive detection"
  - "open science"
  - "benchmark"
abstract: >
  DeepCatch v2.3.0 is an open-source cfDNA fragmentomics benchmark that is
  validated on public data the model has never been trained on. A 5-channel
  DELFI profile (5 Mb / 100 kb ratios and coverage, plus a 196-bin fragment-size
  distribution) is extracted from publicly served FinaleDB fragment files and
  scored with a per-study harmonised PCA + logistic-regression pipeline.
  Training on FinaleDB publications 6 + 8 (n=627) and testing on held-out
  publications 1, 7 and 9 gives an external AUC of 0.9444 [0.8731, 1.0000] on
  one whole-genome publication (n=31), 0.708 [0.5148, 0.9012] on a second
  (n=52), and 0.7385 [0.6281, 0.8488] pooled over the publications carrying
  both classes; the whole-exome publication transfers only partly (breast
  0.6938, prostate 0.7127). Transfer between whole-genome studies is real but
  far from uniform, and the assay platform blunts it further. A leave-one-study-out run inside the
  training cohort gives 0.6805, so the pooled 0.9684 is reported as a
  within-cohort separation number rather than a generalisation claim. The
  multi-modal stage is carried by a regularized late fusion (AUC 0.9825) and a
  Bayesian model average (0.9650) rather than the neural encoder (0.554) at
  this sample size. The methylation path runs end to end on real public
  fragment data. Not clinically validated; research use only.
